[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `SIGPIPE` was ignored, so the Rust program survived a closed stdout
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout wired to a pipe whose read end is already closed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | exit code | signal |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | — | 13 (`SIGPIPE`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before fix) | 0 | — |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout and stderr were both empty in each case, so **a test that compared only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout would have passed.** Only the exit status exposed it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` in its runtime
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m setup before calling `main`. A C program inherits the default disposition. With
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the signal ignored, the failing `write` returns `EPIPE`, the translation's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `let _ = write!(...)` discards that error, and `main` returns normally with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m status 0. The C program is killed by the signal mid-`write`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` calls `signal(SIGPIPE,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m SIG_DFL)` as the first statement of `main`, restoring the disposition the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program runs with.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Regression check.** Commenting out that call makes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `closed_stdout_pipe_raises_sigpipe` fail with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `left: ([], None, Some(13)), right: ([], Some(0), None)`; restoring it makes the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m test pass. The test also pins the absolute expectation (signal 13, not merely
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "both agree"), so it cannot pass by both programs exiting 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. (test defect, not a translation defect) wrong hardcoded expectation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `stdin_from_a_regular_file` initially asserted that `9 4` yields
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `remainder: 0`. The correct value is `remainder: 1`. The two programs agreed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with each other; only my literal was wrong. Corrected in the test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors deliberately preserved, not "fixed"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These all look like bugs and are reproduced exactly. Each was confirmed by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m running the C binary, not inferred from the standard.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` field overflow.** glibc converts a `%d` field with `strtol`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   semantics into a `long`, saturating at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` on overflow, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   only then narrows to `int`. So:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   - `2147483648` becomes `INT_MIN` (`-2147483648`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   - `4294967296` becomes `0` — as a divisor this **traps**, even though the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     written value is nonzero
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   - `9223372036854775807` and any longer digit run become `-1` — as a divisor
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     this turns `-2147483648 / 9999...` into the `INT_MIN / -1` trap
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   - `-9223372036854775808` and anything below it become `0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `Scanner::scan_int` accumulates in `i128`, latches a `saturated` flag once the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   magnitude passes `i64::MAX`, clamps to `i64::MIN`/`i64::MAX`, then casts to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Partial assignment on conversion failure.** `scanf` stops at the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   failing directive and leaves the remaining arguments untouched, so `x` and `y`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   fall back to their initializers `1` and `1`. `quotient: 1, remainder: 0` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the output for empty input, whitespace-only input, and unparsable input alike.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Undefined division traps rather than panicking.** `die_with_sigfpe()` raises
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `SIGFPE` so the process dies by signal 8 with empty stdout, matching the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   hardware trap the C program takes. A Rust panic would have produced a message
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   on stderr and a different exit status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`%d` stops at the first non-digit.** `0x10` parses as `0`, `5.7` as `5`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `5e3` as `5`; `5-2` parses as `5` then `-2`. Whitespace, including newlines, is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   skipped freely by both the literal space in the format and by `%d` itself.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **A lone sign is not a number.** `-`, `+`, `- 5` and `--5` are all matching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   failures, leaving the defaults in place.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`printf` failures are ignored.** With stdout on `/dev/full` the write fails
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and both programs still exit 0. `argv` is ignored by both, since `main()` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   declared with no parameters.
