[1mOne real mismatch found and fixed:[22m the Rust program survived a closed stdout where the C program dies. Rust's std sets [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m to [38;5;10mSIG_IGN[0m before [38;5;10mmain[0m, so the failing [38;5;10mwrite[0m returned [38;5;10mEPIPE[0m, the [38;5;10mlet _ = write!(...)[0m swallowed it, and the process exited 0 — while C was killed by signal 13. Both produced [3mempty stdout and empty stderr[23m, so only the exit status exposed it. Fixed with [38;5;10msignal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)[0m as the first statement of [38;5;10mmain[0m. I confirmed the test catches it: commenting the call out gives [38;5;10mleft: ([], None, Some(13)), right: ([], Some(0), None)[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The rest of the translation was already correct, including the parts that look wrong and must not be fixed — glibc's [38;5;10m%d[0m overflow path ([38;5;10mstrtol[0m saturation at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m, [3mthen[23m narrowing to [38;5;10mint[0m, so [38;5;10m4294967296[0m becomes a trapping zero divisor and [38;5;10m9223372036854775807[0m becomes [38;5;10m-1[0m), partial assignment leaving [38;5;10mx[0m/[38;5;10my[0m at their [38;5;10m1[0m initializers, and [38;5;10mSIGFPE[0m death rather than a Rust panic on [38;5;10mx/0[0m and [38;5;10mINT_MIN / -1[0m.[0m[0m
